---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import Spine from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Spine.astro";
import LINE from "../data/line.json";
/* The line moved to src/data/line.json on 22 September, so the homepage
   ladder reads the same rows. A line that exists twice is a line that
   drifts, and this one has two Petrus Jacobus Booysens in consecutive
   generations — the last place to keep a second copy. */
const line = LINE.line;
/* THE DISPUTE WAS IN THE PROSE AND NOT ON THE ROW. Generation seven is held up
   by one clerk's hand on an 1876 death notice, against a printed genealogy that
   names a different father and agrees only with its own earlier edition. Three
   sections down the page say so at length; the row itself said nothing. The
   shared spine marks the joint where the weight is, which is the whole reason
   this estate draws a spine rather than a pedigree. */
const amp = (t) => String(t ?? "").replace(/&(?!#?\w+;)/g, "&amp;");
const rows = line.map(([g, n, b, d, m, note, m2]) => ({
  gen: g, name: n, spouse: m,
  born: b === "\u2014" ? "" : b,
  died: d === "\u2014" ? "" : d,
  note: amp([note, m2].filter(Boolean).join(" \u00b7 ")),
  weak: g === "7",
  weakNote: g === "7"
    ? `He has no birth date, no death date, no father and no mother. He exists because a
       clerk wrote his name on his son's 1876 death notice - and de Villiers's printed Cape
       genealogy puts that son under a father called <b>Jan</b> instead. One primary document
       signed by a son, against a printed genealogy agreeing with itself.
       <a href="${u("/documents/#question-zero")}">How it was narrowed &rarr;</a>`
    : "",
}));

const sibs = [
  ["Willem Hermanus “Willy”", "15 Oct 1925", "1996"],
  ["Margaretha Wilhelmina Elizabeth “Maggie”", "14 Oct 1926", "1966"],
  ["Catherine Mary Sophia", "25 Mar 1929", "25 Dec 2013"],
  ["Anna Johanna Maria (Vermaak)", "12 Oct 1930", "deceased"],
  ["Georgina Jeanette (Boshoff)", "22 Sep 1932, Johannesburg", "deceased"],
  ["Petrus Jacobus “Piet”", "10 Jan 1934, Johannesburg", "2003"],
  ["Gertrude Elizabeth", "11 May 1946", "15 May 1946 — four days, Queen Victoria Hospital"],
  ["Gertrude", "11 May 1947", "11 May 1947 — six hours, Turf Club Nursing Home"],
];
---
<Base title="The Spine"
      eyebrow="Seven generations, and where they stop"
      dek="From David Defranceski's grandmother back to a man with no dates and no parents. The descent is continuous and the tree is unusually well sourced — but it ends abruptly, and two links inside it are in dispute.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The descent</h2>
  <p class="dek">Generations counted upward from David Defranceski. His mother
    <strong>Cheryl Anne Lerena</strong> (b. 1955) is living and is omitted from this build, as he is.</p>
  <p class="cap">This is the spine only. For everyone else the archive names — siblings, cousins,
    godparents, witnesses and in-laws — see <a href={u("/people/")}>Everyone</a>, which is searchable by
    name, place, year or spelling variant.</p>
  <p class="cap"><b>Two of these men married twice</b>, and in both cases the second wife was found long
    after the first had been written down. Both second marriages are given in the Married column; the
    ancestor chart has one slot for a mother and can only draw one of them.
    <a href={u("/tree/")}>The chart &rarr;</a></p>
  <Spine rows={rows} u={u} />
  <p class="cap">All from the MyHeritage tree <em>Defranceski Family Site (23andMe)</em>, read 9 September
    2026. Most dates there carry a FamilySearch citation to Dutch Reformed Church registers or to Cape and
    Transvaal probate records; those citations are listed on <a href={u("/sources/")}>Sources</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Where it stops, and why that is the whole problem</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Gerthardus Lodewikus Booysen has no birth date, no death date, no father and no mother.</b>
      MyHeritage offers the buttons "Add father" and "Add mother", and there is nothing behind them. Seven
      generations up, the male line simply ends.</p>
    <div class="note">
      <b>One thing has changed here, and it is worth being precise about how little.</b> Until the 1876
      Sterfkennis was read, generation seven existed only because a family tree said so. It now exists
      because a nineteenth-century clerk wrote <b>"Gerthardus L&ouml;dewikus Booijsen"</b> and
      <b>"Magdalena de Beer"</b> on his son's death notice.
      <a href={u("/documents/#sterfkennis-1876")}>See the line &rarr;</a>
      <br /><br />
      <b>15 SEPTEMBER 2026 &mdash; AND THAT CLERK IS NOW THE ONLY THING HOLDING THIS ROW UP.</b>
      De Villiers's printed Cape genealogy of 1893 places this Petrus Jacobus under a father called
      <b>JAN</b>, and Ri&euml;l Smit confirms that <b>de Villiers &amp; Pama&rsquo;s 1981 revision still
      does</b>. Pieter van Zyl searched for a marriage between a Gerhardus Lodewicus and a Magdalena de
      Beer and <b>found none</b>. Jan was dead more than forty years before Petrus Jacobus died. And the
      disputed name sits <b>nine lines lower on the same sheet</b>: line 12 of that notice lists eight
      children and the fifth is <b>JAN GERTHARDUS LODEWIKUS BOOIJSEN</b> &mdash; so the name in the
      parents' box is the back half of a son's name, and the half that was dropped is <em>Jan</em>. The
      man who signed it was Hendrik Stefanus, the seventh child, born about 1828, writing the name of a
      grandfather dead before he was ten.
      <br /><br />
      <b>This row has deliberately not been changed.</b> Everything against him is a printed genealogy
      agreeing with its own earlier edition, which is one source agreeing with itself; what he has is a
      primary document signed by a son. <b>Jan Booijsen is on this site as INFERRED, not documented</b>,
      and he is not in the table above. What ends it is the baptism entry of <b>21 September 1788</b>,
      which is at Cape Town and is not on FamilySearch &mdash; that collection begins in 1843.
      <a href={u("/documents/#question-zero")}>How it was narrowed &rarr;</a>
      <br /><br />
      That converts an assertion into a record. <b>It does not add a generation.</b> The buttons above him
      are still empty, the dates below are still arithmetic, and the question of who his own parents were
      is exactly as open as it was.
    </div>
    <p>What can be said is arithmetic rather than evidence. His wife <b>Magdelena Elisabeth De Beer</b> was
      born in <b>1754</b> and his son in <b>1788</b> — the second of those is now confirmed by an age of
      <b>88</b> on a document of 1876. He was therefore probably born somewhere around
      <b>1745–1760</b> — which places him precisely in the generation that would connect to the
      documented Cape progenitor of the surname.</p>
    <p>That progenitor is known independently of this family. <b>Boij Boijeszoon</b> — in modern spelling
      <b>Boy Booysen</b> — came into Dutch East India Company service from <b>Barlt, in Northern
      Friesland, Schleswig-Holstein</b>, and settled at Stellenbosch and Drakenstein in the mid-seventeenth
      century. <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> Every South African Booysen is presumed to descend
      from him. <b>This family's link to him is not demonstrated here, and must not be assumed.</b>
      <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The other line — through his mother</h2>
  <p class="dek">The male line stops at generation 7 with a man who has no dates. The line through
    <strong>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</strong> runs a generation deeper, and every step of it is now on
    paper.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:16rem">Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th><th>Carried by</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>James Montjoy</strong><div class="cap" style="margin-top:4px">saddler at Cradock;
            his parents recorded as <em>Not Known</em></div></td>
          <td class="m">c. 1782, "Europe"</td><td class="m">Sep 1827, Cradock</td>
          <td>His 1840 death notice, <span class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/21: 4393</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Anna Catharina Sleer</strong><div class="cap" style="margin-top:4px">his wife, m.
            9 Mar 1817 Graaff-Reinet; previously widow of William Pietersen</div></td>
          <td class="m">1787</td><td class="m">1885 — aged 98</td>
          <td>The 1840 death notice; the 1817 marriage index</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</strong><div class="cap" style="margin-top:4px">m. Petrus
            Jacobus Booysen, 4 Nov 1838 Cradock</div></td>
          <td class="m">30 Apr 1819, Graaff-Reinet</td><td class="m">19 May 1863, Zevenfontein</td>
          <td>Her 1863 death notice, <span class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/104: 499</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</strong><div class="cap" style="margin-top:4px">generation 4
            of the line above — her fifth child</div></td>
          <td class="m">4 May 1851, achter Sneeuberg</td><td class="m">13 Sep 1905, Indwe</td>
          <td>His 1851 baptism; his mother's 1863 death notice</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p style="margin:0">Her age on the 1863 form is given as <b>"44 years &amp; 19 days"</b> when the form
      asked only for years — which fixes her birth at <b>30 April 1819</b>. And <b>Willem Hermanus is the
      fifth of the eight children listed on that form</b>, which is how the Mountjoy descent enters this
      family: <b>through his mother, not his father</b>.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px">This line stops for a different reason than the Booysen one. It does not run
    out of records — it runs out of memory. Asked for James Montjoy's parents in 1840, thirteen years after
    his death, a relative who knew his trade, his house and its value wrote <b>"Not Known."</b>
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The 1840 notice &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<Rule name="koppie" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>Two links inside the line are in dispute</h2>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Willem Hermanus — now settled</h3>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The tree doubted its own date. The
        <b>1905 death registration</b> confirms it: he died at <b>Doorn Street, Indwe, on 13 September
        1905</b>, of a <b>stomach ulcer</b> after fourteen months' illness. His son <b>David Schalk,
        recorded born 1907</b>, therefore still cannot be his — that child, not the date, is the error.
        <a href={u("/documents/")}>The document &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Johanna Catharina died when?</h3>
      <p>Her death at <b>Zevenfontein on 19 May 1863</b> is supported by a probate record. But
        <b>Izak (1864)</b> and <b>Stephanus Francois (1875)</b> are both recorded as her sons. They cannot
        be. <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> Petrus Jacobus almost certainly
        <b>remarried, and the second wife is missing from the tree entirely.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px">Neither breaks the descent to David — both sit on side branches — but both
    are warnings about how the tree was assembled. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>The full list &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Catherine's brothers and sisters</h2>
  <p class="dek">The children of George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen — six by Anna Pretorius, then two by
    Gertruida Kinnear.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22rem">Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{sibs.map(([n, b, d]) => (<tr><td>{n}</td><td class="m">{b}</td><td class="m">{d}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    Read the last two rows slowly. After Anna died in February 1944, George remarried. A daughter
    <b>Gertrude Elizabeth</b> was born on <b>11 May 1946</b> and died four days later at the
    <b>Queen Victoria Hospital</b>, of prematurity at about thirty weeks. A second daughter,
    <b>Gertrude</b>, was born on <b>11 May 1947</b> — the same day of the year — and lived
    <b>six hours</b>, at the <b>Turf Club Nursing Home</b>. <span class="chip doc">Both certificates
    read</span> They are two different children: two hospitals, two doctors, two cemeteries, two
    registration entries a year apart. <b>The shared birthday is real coincidence, not a duplicated
    record.</b> The household lost a mother, two infants and a father inside three years and ten months.
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The certificates &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
